00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.000
Well, so I know that CoreOS
has a very popular, definitely

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.562
here at Google,
product called at etcd.

00:05:01.562 --> 00:05:03.520
Can you tell us a little
bit about what that is

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.044
and what it's used for
and where people use it?

00:05:06.044 --> 00:05:06.960
BRANDON PHILIPS: Sure.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.280
So etcd is a consensus
key value store.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.680
For a lot of folks, that is
a large mouthful of words.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.430
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.138
BRANDON PHILIPS: But
really what it means

00:05:15.138 --> 00:05:18.237
is that a set of machines
inside of a data center--

00:05:18.237 --> 00:05:19.820
let's say you have
five machines-- are

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.920
able to store data.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.370
They will do it consistently,
and if one of those machines

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:29.430
dies or loses power or
whatever, the key values

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.810
that were stored there are
in the exact same state,

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.632
replicated in the exact same
way, on the other machines.

00:05:34.632 --> 00:05:36.340
And this is really
important, because you

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.771
want to start to do things--
when you have lots of machines,

00:05:39.771 --> 00:05:41.270
you want to make a
decision, and you

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.060
want to ensure that
decision is replicated

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.900
and consistent across
all the machines.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.630
So you want to
take a lock or you

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:50.000
want to update some load
balancer configuration

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.932
or you want to update
some DNS configuration,

00:05:52.932 --> 00:05:54.640
you want to ensure
that there is only one

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.270
true configuration
of the cluster,

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.310
particularly as you scale
up to lots of machines

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:00.924
inside of your infrastructure.

00:06:00.924 --> 00:06:01.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.190
So I have an extra
question about etcd,

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:08.420
which is what does it mean?

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:09.400
BRANDON PHILIPS: Sure.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.190
So we were trying to be
really cute with the name.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:16.410
So the idea is that you hold
onto really important cluster

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.630
configuration,
but it's a DAEMON.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.290
It's like a system service.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:24.936
And so slash etc on a single
machine-- like E-T-C--

00:06:24.936 --> 00:06:27.660
is where you hold onto
configuration of a single host,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.500
but in the case of
a DAEMON, you're

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.690
going to want to hold
onto that same data,

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.918
so it's etc DAEMON-- etcd.

00:06:33.918 --> 00:06:35.334
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That makes sense.

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:36.584
Cool.

00:06:36.584 --> 00:06:37.500
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.734
Product marketing wasn't
a strong suit of CoreOS

00:06:40.734 --> 00:06:42.980
when we were four people.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:44.620
MARK MANDEL: And
since coming from me,

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:45.750
it will seem less biased.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.040
What language is
etcd written in?

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.150
BRANDON PHILIPS: etcd
is written in Go.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:50.900
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:51.816
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:51.816 --> 00:06:54.670
That's actually how I heard
about etcd for the first time.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:58.270
And then some time
ago, I saw a talk

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:02.975
where you explained how etcd
was moving from REST to gRPC.

00:07:02.975 --> 00:07:03.850
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yes.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:08.590
So etcd, when we
started to build etcd,

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:11.500
one of the goals-- we had three
goals-- one of the goals was we

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.140
wanted to make distributed
systems easy to consume,

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.340
and that goal was
achieved by using REST.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.380
We wanted to make it so that
it was runtime reconfigurable

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.210
so it fits into these
cloud environments,

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:24.585
so you can add
and remove members

00:07:24.585 --> 00:07:27.260
from a cluster and
that sort of thing.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:30.580
And then we wanted to build
it using modern technologies

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.400
and modern languages like Go.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.380
And so we started with REST as
an easy-to-consume endpoint.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.740
But as etcd started to
get adopted by projects

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.130
like Kubernetes, which have
larger and larger scaling

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:51.070
requirements, the REST decision
ended up becoming a bottleneck.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:54.910
It started to show up as one
of the factors in the scaling

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:56.960
and performance of etcd.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.130
And so we started looking
around at various solutions,

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:04.810
and gRPC was open
sourced about, I guess,

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.690
18 months ago-- at least when
we started to look at it.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:10.140
And so we'd already
started using protobuf

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.480
as the internal protocol and
kind of layering on htb2.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.630
And protobuf on the client
protocol as an option

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:22.800
was pretty compelling
in order to flatten out

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.951
that kind of growth in
memory and CPU consumption

00:08:24.951 --> 00:08:27.200
that we were seeing as we
were trying to scale etcd up

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.672
to large Kubernetes workloads.

00:08:31.672 --> 00:08:33.130
MARK MANDEL: So
that's interesting.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:37.409
What sort of scaling or
performance increases

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:39.834
were you able to see
moving over to gRPC?

00:08:39.834 --> 00:08:40.750
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.660
So we saw several, like
10-- well, let's see.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:49.680
It was like 2 to 5x on
memory and CPU improvements

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.940
by moving over.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.680
And, really, it just
comes down to etcd

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:56.480
has a lot of complex
data structures

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.620
that get encoded to JSON.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:04.260
And so just simply moving to a
format that's more efficient,

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.220
like protobuf, causes
just less chatter

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.400
on the wire, less bytes when
you're trying to encode it,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.330
and that sort of thing.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:17.010
So it's really a pretty
straightforward, understandable

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.040
how we got there with
the improvements.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.940
And then htb2, we were
able to see really

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.400
a big concern in these
databases is file descriptors.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.800
So as you get more and more
users of your database, which

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.770
is great, there are
natural constraints

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:36.780
of an operating system.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.613
And one of those things
is file descriptors.

00:09:38.613 --> 00:09:41.720
And so being able to multiplex
lots and lots of requests

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:45.510
from a single client
onto a single TCP stream

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:49.460
was a nice win on actually
the inside of the CPU memory

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:54.460
side, the operating system
networking side of the system.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:55.220
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.910
And is this
available now in etcd

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:00.360
or is this something that's
coming in a future version?

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.850
What's the timeline there?

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.300
BRANDON PHILIPS:
Yeah, so etcd v3,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.730
which was released
about three months ago,

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:11.110
introduced the v3 API, which is
accessible as both the REST API

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.110
and it is a gRPC API.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.120
So it's available today.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.970
It's being used in
a lot of projects.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:21.680
We did upstream work in
Kubernetes to enable v3 usage.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.109
And then because gRPC has all
these great language bindings,

00:10:25.109 --> 00:10:26.650
we're actually
starting to see things

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:30.760
like a Java client
come out for etcd v3

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.140
and a bunch of other
clients that kind of got

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:34.850
built automatically.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:37.770
So it's been a
nice adoption curve

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.584
over the last two or three
months as we released etcd v3.

00:10:41.584 --> 00:10:42.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:49.700
So you mentioned that the v3
API is both in gRPC and REST.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:51.304
How do you manage that?

00:10:51.304 --> 00:10:52.220
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:55.850
So the way that we
do that is there's

00:10:55.850 --> 00:11:00.530
a really great project that
is called the gRPC Gateway.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.400
And the cool thing about gRPC
is that it's very declarative,

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:09.550
and it declares essentially
the verb, the noun,

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.540
the nouns like what you're
actually operating on.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:16.840
And using this gRPC
Gateway Library,

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.250
you're able to flatten that
down into a REST endpoint.

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:22.780
So you're able to say,
well, this function

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:25.410
call that I'm going
to have in gRPC,

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:26.920
it's acting on this resource.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:30.030
And I want to map this
function call to put or post

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.470
on this resource.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.840
And the resource
name ends up becoming

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.990
arguments or the resource path
ends up becoming arguments

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:38.130
to the function call.

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:42.090
And so you're able to flatten
very nicely gRPC into REST.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:46.200
And so this gateway, what
it does is it will expose,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:47.840
not just a REST
endpoint that maps

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:51.600
through gRPC Gateway, but
also an open API, which

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.399
was previously known
as Swagger, but it'll

00:11:54.399 --> 00:11:55.565
give you a Swagger document.

00:11:55.565 --> 00:11:57.890
So you can consume it in
a variety of languages

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:00.860
that already know how to
speak to Swagger-enabled REST

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:01.710
endpoints.

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:05.550
So it's a quite powerful
little framework

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.900
if you want to start building
internet accessible APIs.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:12.950
You get all the best of
the world REST availability

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.340
and then the efficiencies and
memory efficiencies of gRPC

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.557
for clients that care
highly about that.

00:12:18.557 --> 00:12:20.140
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
curious, it sounds

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.990
like you're using gRPC and
REST for like externals

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.370
or like API calls
from external parties.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.600
Does it use it for
internal communications

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.214
as well between etcd servers?

00:12:31.214 --> 00:12:32.130
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:36.610
So inside of etcd, before we
even started to look at gRPC,

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:40.760
we had already
moved to a streaming

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.060
protobuf-based
protocol internally.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:47.370
So essentially, it's
already HTTP plus protobuf

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:50.840
on the wire as the content type.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.820
And so since the etcd
machines-- there's

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:58.430
only like five or seven of
them in a cluster, essentially

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.770
as maximum-- we're
able to stream really

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.470
efficiently with single
HTTP connections internally

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:04.690
and already using protobuf.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:09.320
So we sort of have a
pseudo-gRPC protocol internally.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:12.490
And we haven't really
had a need to redesign

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:14.510
that because of the
streaming nature

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.394
of how etcd actually
does its work.

00:13:18.394 --> 00:13:19.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:24.630
Is it efficient to expose the
REST APIs through that gateway?

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:26.640
Does it actually
make it much slower

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.230
or is it around the same speed
as actually exposing REST

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:30.014
directly?

00:13:30.014 --> 00:13:30.930
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.930
So there are two considerations.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:36.050
The first consideration
is that we

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:38.580
do have to do a little bit of
packet sniffing to figure out,

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:41.540
is this an HTTP/1
REST request or is

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:44.530
this an HTTP/2 gRPC request?

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:46.480
That introduces a
tiny bit of latency

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.740
on the initial
connection, but it's

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.030
a constant time small latency.

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:53.670
So we really don't worry about
that and a couple extra memory

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.610
copies.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:58.080
As far as the efficiency,
yeah, essentially

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.870
by using the REST
endpoint, you lose

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:04.480
a lot of the efficiencies
of the new gRPC API.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.260
So you go back to including
and decoding JSON,

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:13.450
and you lose the ability to say
multiplex multiple streaming

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.500
requests over the same socket.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:19.630
So you have that TLS bring
up time and tear down time.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:21.530
So it is quite a
bit more efficient,

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:25.897
that same 2 to 5x efficiency,
by using the gRPC endpoint.

00:14:25.897 --> 00:14:26.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:28.290
So at the end of
the day, basically,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.680
what you do is you
expose a gRPC, and people

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:34.230
that care about efficiency,
they will use that one.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:38.100
And you are basically
getting REST almost for free

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.711
for anyone else that wants
the use Swagger or Open API.

00:14:40.711 --> 00:14:41.752
BRANDON PHILIPS: Exactly.

00:14:41.752 --> 00:14:42.516
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:42.516 --> 00:14:44.391
BRANDON PHILIPS: And
there's a huge ecosystem

00:14:44.391 --> 00:14:45.930
around Swagger and Open API--

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:46.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:48.110
BRANDON PHILIPS: --and
so it's nice to be

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.520
able to bridge that world.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:54.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I know
that consuming any API that

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:56.904
exposes a Swagger
definition is very simple,

00:14:56.904 --> 00:14:58.320
and there are many
tools for that.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.130
What about a gRPC API?

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:01.924
How do you get started?

00:15:01.924 --> 00:15:02.840
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:06.960
So getting started with this
is pretty straightforward.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:10.960
So there are
protobuf definitions

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.470
for-- or gRPC
definitions, I guess,

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.840
for etcd that explain
all the function calls

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.590
into the remote API.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:23.860
And then there are
various code generators

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:27.280
that will automatically
create a client binding.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:30.149
So what we've done
inside of etcd was

00:15:30.149 --> 00:15:31.940
we have a Go client
binding, and then we've

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:37.525
wrapped that generated code into
a slightly more nice for humans

00:15:37.525 --> 00:15:39.530
to consume API.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:41.550
And we're doing
similar work for Java.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:44.860
But if folks want to build,
they can just straight

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.930
CodeGen and use the
CodeGen directly.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:52.090
Or if they want to kind
of wrap it up or super

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:55.780
eager to help people
make easy-to-consume and

00:15:55.780 --> 00:16:00.460
human-consumable APIs
for other languages,

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:01.615
the team is eager to help.

00:16:01.615 --> 00:16:02.990
MARK MANDEL: Did
you find you had

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.230
to do much work from
the generated code

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:07.904
to kind of make it
pretty, so to speak, or--

00:16:07.904 --> 00:16:08.820
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:09.820
That's a great question.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.140
So we did a few things.

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:15.470
So etcd has some
transactional semantics.

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:17.690
So we kind of have
transactions where

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:22.830
if this statement holds
true in the database,

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.810
then execute this transaction.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:27.750
If the statement is not true,
then do this other thing.

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:31.230
And you usually have to do a
little bit of semantic work

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:36.150
with closures in et cetera in
order to get that to look nice.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:39.050
So people can totally
do gets and puts

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:44.500
and the basics of a key value
store with the raw gRPC client,

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:47.150
but once you want to get
into like many transactions

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:48.880
and that sort of
thing, you want to have

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.840
something that saves you
from some of the boilerplate

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.100
annoyances.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I kind of want
to ask about the streaming API.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.085
Are using it?

00:16:57.085 --> 00:16:57.960
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yes.

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:02.810
So etcd v3 has a couple
of streaming endpoints.

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:07.730
The most used or one of the
biggest use cases of etcd

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:12.290
is to actually get a change,
essentially stream of changes,

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:14.470
of all the keys and values.

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:17.829
So let's talk through, say,
how Kubernetes uses this.

00:17:17.829 --> 00:17:22.582
So Kubernetes uses etcd
as its primary data store.

00:17:22.582 --> 00:17:24.540
A bunch of different
things are happening, say,

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:27.660
service discovery
information is changing,

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.560
resources are getting
updated through the REST API.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:33.770
And you have all these things
that may be caching that data

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:38.330
and want to be invalidated
when something changes.

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:41.390
And so by keeping this
TCB connection open,

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:44.080
you can create these really
dynamic systems based on etcd

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:47.000
where key changes,
and then everyone who

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:48.800
cares about that key
change is immediately

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:51.300
notified without any polling.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.880
And the internal
protocol of etcd

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.730
allows for this to be
kind of a safe operation.

00:17:57.730 --> 00:18:00.220
You can kind think of
etcd as being like SVN

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:01.770
for your key value stores.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:05.970
So if you remember like
SVN, the revision system,

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.220
every time you made a change,
it incremented the revision

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:09.860
by one.

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:13.610
And so etcd keeps this similar
index or revision internally.

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:15.370
And so you say,
I want all things

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.250
that have happened
since revision X,

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:19.162
and then it streams
you back all that.

00:18:19.162 --> 00:18:21.120
And then once you get to
the end of the stream,

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.030
it just keeps the
stream open, and it

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:28.630
will send you the next stream
from X plus N, wherever

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:30.060
you left off.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:31.810
MARK MANDEL: So that's
really interesting.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:33.520
So what did you do before gRPC?

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:35.000
How did you stream that data?

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:35.916
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:18:35.916 --> 00:18:39.440
So this is one of the biggest
inefficiencies of etcd

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:43.060
with the REST endpoint was that
we used REST's long polling.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:46.300
So you would say I
want everything that's

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:50.450
happened since X, and you
would make an HTTP connection,

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:52.800
and we'd stream
everything back since X

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:54.660
and then close the connection.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:58.484
And then if you said X and
X hasn't happened yet--

00:18:58.484 --> 00:18:59.900
this revision
hadn't happened yet.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:03.590
It's in the future-- we would
just not return an HTTP body.

00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:06.830
And once X incremented,
then we would

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:09.591
return you the HTTP body
with all the changes

00:19:09.591 --> 00:19:11.090
and then close the
connection again.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:13.173
And so this is a very
inefficient way of doing it,

00:19:13.173 --> 00:19:17.050
but it's essentially how
the entire internet works

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:19.570
today are these extremely
inefficient, constantly

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.167
closing HTTP requests.

00:19:22.167 --> 00:19:23.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.300
That's sounds
really interesting.

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:31.290
So I'm wondering why did
you choose gRPC for etcd,

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:34.780
if it was because you had used
it for something else before

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:37.400
or it was just
because it was faster?

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:39.994
And are you using it
now for other things?

00:19:39.994 --> 00:19:40.910
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yeah.

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:46.510
So we ended up choosing gRPC
for essentially two reasons.

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:49.570
One is that we were interested
in HTTP/2 already because

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:51.700
of this long polling thing.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:54.230
We were pretty dissatisfied
with tearing down

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:57.067
these connections-- these
TLS connections-- every time.

00:19:57.067 --> 00:19:58.650
And then we had
already had experience

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:01.030
in using protobuf
internally in etcd

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:03.470
and were super comfortable
with the tooling.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:06.980
And so gRPC was a
pretty natural fit.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:09.500
There are also some
legacy reasons.

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:13.280
CoreOS was a very, very early
user of Google App Engine,

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:17.927
and we essentially
had built gRPC

00:20:17.927 --> 00:20:20.010
based on a bunch of internal
stuff from Google App

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:22.186
Engine once we had to move
off of Google App Engine.

00:20:22.186 --> 00:20:23.560
And so we were
really comfortable

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:28.580
with this REST proxy model
of gRPC Gateway and protobuf

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:31.950
from our experience
almost three years ago.

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:34.760
So it was essentially comfort
with the tooling and confidence

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:37.640
in the tooling that
caused us to choose gRPC

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:40.850
over a lot of the alternatives
that are out there today.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:43.170
And then the other
big reason was

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:45.290
we wanted to do
this CodeGen stuff,

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:46.890
and we saw that
gRPC was building

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:50.130
a really solid ecosystem of
client binding generation

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:51.020
tools.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:53.480
And we wanted to see if
we could plug into that,

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:57.990
and that's starting, like I said
earlier, to come to fruition.

00:20:57.990 --> 00:20:58.740
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:00.600
And to get to Francesc's
earlier question,

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.226
are you using it for
any other projects

00:21:02.226 --> 00:21:04.141
or do you plan on using
it for other projects?

00:21:04.141 --> 00:21:05.040
BRANDON PHILIPS: Yes.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:08.220
So we use gRPC in a couple
of different projects.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.000
Rocket is a Container
Engine that we

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:14.450
built and have started to work
with a bunch of other folks

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:15.250
on since then.

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.600
And Rocket has an
API service that

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:20.930
exposes a bunch of
information on the host.

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:23.040
So this is stuff
like what images

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.770
are available for running?

00:21:24.770 --> 00:21:27.600
What are the current statuses
of the containers running

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.560
on the host, that sort of thing.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:32.620
And so we use gRPC
in that service.

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.530
And this is one
of the components

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:36.030
that we plug into
Kubernetes-- where

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:37.450
we plug Rocket into Kubernetes.

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:42.310
So Kubernetes is using gRPC
to talk to Rocket today.

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:45.590
And then all of the
internal customer billing

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:49.370
systems for tectonic.com
is all based on gRPC.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:52.660
As soon as we started to
use gRPC in our open source

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:55.640
projects, a lot of
our back office stuff,

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.870
nobody wanted to build
another REST API.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.990
So it started to be adopted
pretty naturally on all

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:03.274
of our back office work too.

00:22:03.274 --> 00:22:04.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:06.027
That really sounds
like maybe we should

00:22:06.027 --> 00:22:07.610
have your back for
a different episode

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:10.100
and have you talk about all
of those different products.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.760
Those sound really cool too.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:12.060
BRANDON PHILIPS: Cool.

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:12.560
Yeah.

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:13.780
Would be happy to do it.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:16.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I
have one more question,

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:20.380
which is, OK, so
if you did the--

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:25.870
how do you say the word-- the
migration from REST to gRPC,

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:29.022
are there any lessons
that you learned

00:22:29.022 --> 00:22:30.230
that you would like to share?

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:33.372
Any painful story
or success story?

00:22:33.372 --> 00:22:35.830
BRANDON PHILIPS: So I think
probably the most painful thing

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:40.460
was we pretty rapidly learned
by building a key value

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:43.720
database that people wanted
to store binary data.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:46.300
And binary data is
really, really hard

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:49.625
to store on a REST API,
particularly key value REST

00:22:49.625 --> 00:22:53.430
API, because keys are
really hard to represent.

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:55.840
You end up base64-ing
everything.

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:57.600
And then the values,
you end up having

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:01.660
to somehow generally
base64 or something worse

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.520
into JSON bodies.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:07.760
And so gRPC, because it
has like a native byte type

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:09.830
and can be encoded
into protobufs

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:12.630
has been a pretty big win there.

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:18.520
And then just I think people
underestimate the cost of JSON

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:19.740
in a lot of their systems.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.870
I always joke that the
heat death of the universe

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:25.000
will be caused by
the last JSON being

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:30.650
decoded, just because it's so
expensive to encode and decode

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.190
JSON.

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:35.050
There are tricks that you can
do to make it more efficient,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:37.946
but it does take a lot
of CPU memory to do it.

00:23:37.946 --> 00:23:39.320
And if you're
doing it a lot, you

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:43.700
need to consider if there are
more effective ways of doing

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:44.340
it.

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:44.600
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:45.194
