of easy with App Engine,

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:40.680
because you basically
get this fake DataStore

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.240
thing that it's running there.

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:44.240
But what happens if
you're not App Engine?

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:47.160
What if you're using just
the cloud DataStore API?

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:49.160
MARK MANDEL: Oh, and I
might be using like a GKE

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:49.870
instance or something?

00:25:49.870 --> 00:25:50.786
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:50.786 --> 00:25:52.200
Like you're not on App Engine.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:55.070
Well, before it was
kind of hard to do it.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:25:59.180
You needed to basically have
some way of faking a server

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:00.480
or something like that.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:03.635
And we fixed that by providing
a DataStore emulator.

00:26:03.635 --> 00:26:04.343
MARK MANDEL: Ooh.

00:26:04.343 --> 00:26:05.259
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:05.259 --> 00:26:07.060
So it is super simple to use.

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:12.270
If you're using any
of the G Cloud SDKs,

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:15.040
which are available for
many different languages,

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:18.300
but basically you can find
like gcloud-golang, gcloud-node

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.130
and all of those.

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:22.930
There are a bunch of
environment variables

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:25.390
that you can set
in your environment

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:28.292
to make your code
point to those instead

00:26:28.292 --> 00:26:30.250
to the production for
that kind of environment.

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:31.375
MARK MANDEL: Oh, very nice.

00:26:31.375 --> 00:26:34.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And to
